---
/**
 * SKETCHES, 26 Nov 2026, later (a founder: "verify please all the copy and their sketch and see if
 * the copy aligns with the sketch"): the house-front drawings matched the old copy, not these words, so
 * every section now has sketches of the joinery its words describe (src/data/area-drawings.ts, the
 * 26 Nov block; AreaSide takes a list where the words name two pieces): alcove wardrobes and alcove shelves; attic walk-in; wall-to-wall Shaker wardrobe and hall coat cupboard; mews cut-through and TV unit; wardrobe below the cornice; a wardrobe section up a protected stair.
 *
 * REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: the remaining
 * area pages rewritten to what Kaspar comments on). Every body line is new, in
 * his register: no council or history narration and no source in a sentence
 * (D7), no "bespoke carpentry" (D3), headings name the fitting, the room and
 * the place (K17), listed homes and access on the guide's templates (K18, K19).
 * Gone: Tidey, every date, Camden's statement, Grade II, the Article 4
 * paragraph, the lift-booking line (protocol conduct fact 6, not his), and the
 * FAQ "Do you need planning permission for a walk-in wardrobe?" (it spoke for
 * the council, which he cut on Islington, and repeated the listed section). Its
 * place goes to "Should you have a door on a walk-in wardrobe?" (Google People
 * also ask, walk in wardrobe, UK, built-in browser, 26 Nov 2026), with one Ask.
 * Prices section untouched (no comment from him yet: silence, not approval).
 * FACT LIST (each claim, then its source):
 *   - Products and links: the service pages. Belsize Square, Belsize Village:
 *     facts file section 1 (S1 core villa streets; sub area two).
 *   - Villas on Belsize Park Gardens, Buckland Crescent, Belsize Square, in
 *     pairs: facts file section 1 (S1, villa type). Alcoves beside a chimney
 *     breast are the facts file's inference, so the page says "If".
 *   - A wardrobe in each alcove, each sized to its own recess: his facts table
 *     (front and back alcove units, each recess measured separately, 23 Nov
 *     Camden; alcoves differ in width or depth, 26 Nov Islington).
 *   - Low cupboards with open shelves above: his alcove cabinets answer
 *     (26 Nov, Islington FAQ).
 *   - Some villas have an attic: facts file section 1 ("sometimes an attic").
 *     Attic rooms work well for a walk-in; hanging space, drawers, shoe storage
 *     and shelving planned together: his walk-in wardrobe opening (6 Sep 2026,
 *     service-copy.ts). Rails where the ceiling is highest, low drawers under
 *     the slope: facts table (loft rooms, 23 Nov Camden, 26 Nov Islington).
 *     Rail heights and drawer and shelf splits: the walk-in wardrobe page.
 *   - Manor Mansions, Antrim Mansions, Hillfield Court, Tudor Close: facts
 *     file section 1 (mansion flats). Wall to wall: facts table (23 Nov
 *     Camden). A cupboard made to fit a recess: his alcove cabinets answer.
 *     Shaker or handleless: his Hampstead mansion flats line (8 Nov; K11).
 *     Painted or oak veneer: the finishes on the price cards and service pages.
 *   - Mews north of Belsize Lane, Daleham Mews, two storeys, windows on one
 *     side only: facts file section 1 (S1 sub area two). Built-in TV unit: his
 *     6 Sep price review, the service page.
 *   - Homes on Belsize Grove and Belsize Lane are listed: facts file section 1
 *     (26 to 38 Belsize Grove, 73 to 93 Belsize Lane). Camden's listed
 *     buildings page has a map to check (read 26 Nov 2026). Ask the council
 *     about consent, skirting and panelling: facts table (26 Nov Islington).
 *     Finish below a cornice: facts table (10 Nov Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden).
 *   - Unloading, parking and the route agreed before fitting day; a trade
 *     permit where needed; the customer asked for lift and access details;
 *     the managing agent, insurance and safety documents; sections assembled
 *     in the room for steps and narrow stairs; floors and stairs protected:
 *     facts table (8 and 10 Nov Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden). Who arranges the
 *     permit: 26 Nov Islington (K19). Zone and hours by street or postcode:
 *     Camden's parking zones page (read 26 Nov 2026). Steps up to the front
 *     door: facts file section 1 (villa type, porticos).
 *   - FAQs: dressing room, the cost guide; mirrors, glass, metal details and
 *     specialist handles, the walk-in page; a larger project, time depends on
 *     the room and specification, and the inside on show, the walk-in page's
 *     own leads (6 Sep). Floor space and the door trade-offs: ours, plain
 *     reasoning, no claim about what we do. Which doors he fits: Ask.
 *
 * Belsize Park, 14 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review
 * link (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaBelsizeParkCheck until he has read it and his changes are in.
 *
 * Structure and sources: ops repo research/area-pages-2026-11-14/README.md
 * (Belsize Park), every local fact from facts-belsize-park-2026-11-14.md:
 * Camden's Belsize Conservation Area Statement (the 2002 statement, read
 * 14 Nov 2026), Camden's pages on Article 4 Directions, listed buildings and
 * trade permits, controlled parking zones and the Planning Portal's internal
 * alterations page, all read 14 Nov 2026. Camden's three June 2026 Belsize
 * documents (the Article 4 Direction, fact sheet and design guide) could not
 * be read, so the page says only that the direction exists, is aimed at
 * outside work, and where to check.
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. From what he approved: the walk-in wardrobe page (his opening,
 * 6 Sep 2026: hanging space, drawers, shoe storage and shelving planned
 * together to make the best use of the available space; sloping ceilings and
 * awkward angles incorporated rather than simply boxed off; rail heights from
 * what hangs, drawer and shelf splits from what folds; mirrors, glass, metal
 * details and specialist handles; the larger project and the inside on show),
 * the guarantee, and the fitted wardrobe and alcove price lines as approved on
 * Hampstead. The visit and survey lines (q07, q18, q14, q17) are no longer on
 * this page. Two conduct lines: the lift line, the protocol's approved conduct
 * fact (section 11, item 6), and parking and deliveries agreed before
 * installation, as on Primrose Hill and Camden. Everything else is new copy
 * for him to check.
 *
 * Street and building names are Camden's statement's, describing the houses.
 * None is a place we claim to have worked. No review block: no quotable
 * review names Belsize Park, and the rating line sits under the hero buttons
 * already.
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL, 15 Nov 2026 (a founder's go; Denis: "use the
 * same design and structure for the next articles"; ops README, "The
 * structure rule, applied"): the address moved to /areas/belsize-park/; the
 * title, heading, kicker and description lead with bespoke wardrobes, the
 * area's one product search in Search Console, then walk-in wardrobes, the
 * register's photographs (areas.ts); the opening carries "bespoke carpentry"
 * and "bespoke joinery" once each; a line drawing beside each section that
 * describes something (src/data/area-drawings.ts: a pair of villas about one
 * shared chimney stack, one wall of an open walk-in wardrobe, a 1930s block of
 * flats among trees, a row of mews houses), none where a section is rules
 * only; the guide prices as cards; register photos and one labelled AI image
 * (ssp-008, cropped to 3:2, AI painted wardrobes by a bay window, Figma node
 * 33:2, and since 21 Nov 2026 an AI attic walk-in wardrobe in place of ssp-006,
 * which showed the same walk-in as ssp-008; the reasons are in areas.ts). New: a walk-in
 * wardrobe section, built on Camden's line that the villas were built with
 * attic and basement space for servants. Gone: the cornice line (Camden's page
 * has it too) and the measuring line in the opening (Primrose Hill's is close
 * to it); the lift line moved from the questions into the mansion flats
 * section.
 *
 * BROUGHT TO THE UPDATED COPY RULES, 19 Nov 2026 (a founder, in chat: "lock
 * islington, move next"). Written on 15 Nov, before the newsroom playbook and
 * the area page rules v1.0 (17 Nov). Four independent checks (copy rules with
 * the playbook, facts, the swap test against all nine other pages, and its AI
 * picture under the founders' 19 Nov picture rules: no LEDs, no plants on the
 * joinery, a handle on every door, nothing that reads as AI) found, and this
 * change fixes:
 *   - the opening: answers the search first; its second paragraph is Belsize
 *     Park's own (Tidey Town, Camden's statement, S1), not Swiss Cottage's or
 *     Highgate's "drawn for its room"; the nickname left the villas section;
 *   - building terms explained (the bay, the chimney stack, the mews' windows,
 *     stock brick, Grade II, Article 4); no "stucco";
 *   - Camden named in the sentence for the listed-building rule, the walk-in
 *     line and the mews dates; the bay as "typically" (S1 gives the type);
 *   - the access lines: "can affect" (an inference), no section-size method,
 *     the promise as on the other pages ("Parking and deliveries", no
 *     "access"), "the zone for your street";
 *   - the mansion flats section: a heading of its own, the Hillfield House
 *     history trimmed, one line on fit in a flat (q31); "makes good use" for
 *     the mews (no unsourced superlative); the prices heading its own;
 *   - the AI plate's alt text: sage, one cream curtain, no phrase shared with
 *     other pages. The picture itself passed all the rules; no remake.
 *   A recheck of the changed lines then fixed: the Belsize Lane terrace's
 *   source, an opening that matched West Hampstead's, and the alcoves and
 *   attics line made conditional as in the body.
 *   Left as they were: the price strings (the service pages' own, which verify
 *   checks), the guarantee wording (open for a founder), "Parking and
 *   deliveries are agreed" (as on Primrose Hill and Camden; not in Kaspar's
 *   conduct list), the planning FAQ's "internal work" reading of the Portal.
 *
 * CHECKED 15 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against the
 * facts file and approved text, the swap test against Camden, Primrose Hill,
 * Islington and Hampstead). Changed on their findings: the opening's second
 * sentence cut to twelve words; the bay seat, listed-building, listed check,
 * access and parking lines reworded (they were close to Primrose Hill's or
 * Camden's); the van clause and a method line in the flats section gone (not
 * approved conduct facts); the managed-block line says "can"; his walk-in
 * lines restored word for word; Baynes Mews dropped (its frontage is three
 * storeys); the fitted wardrobe and alcove cards take the approved Hampstead
 * lines, with the alcove basis; the first answer no longer touches price (the
 * cards do); the planning answer says only what the Planning Portal says; the
 * third answer opens with one line of ours on floor space, for him to check,
 * then his two lines; the ssp-006 alt no longer names a hanging bay.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new: "What is a walk-in wardrobe called?" and "What
 * are the disadvantages of a walk-in wardrobe?" (Google People also ask, walk
 * in wardrobe), "Do you need planning permission for a walk-in wardrobe?"
 * (People also ask on walk in wardrobes belsize park, walk in wardrobe and
 * walk in wardrobes london); UK, English, desktop, the built-in browser,
 * 15 Nov 2026. The box for bespoke wardrobes belsize park itself held only
 * costs and other companies' names (the price cards answer the one, no
 * comparisons for the other), as did bespoke wardrobes, bespoke wardrobes
 * london and built in wardrobes. Answers from what he approved: the "dressing
 * room" name from the cost guide and the walk-in page's line on mirrors,
 * glass, metal details and handles; the Planning Portal's internal alterations
 * page (facts file, S5), with the listed section above carrying the
 * exception, so the section no longer opens with the planning sentence; the
 * walk-in wardrobe page's how-long lead and its made-from lead (the inside on
 * show). Dropped: the lift and
 * survey questions (not from a product search; the lift answer lives on in
 * the mansion flats section) and "Do carpenters do built-in wardrobes?" (a
 * carpenter question).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";
import Ask from "../../components/Ask.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/belsize-park")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "walk-in-wardrobes",
    name: "Walk-in wardrobes",
    href: "/walk-in-wardrobes/",
    price: "Typically from around £5,000.",
    moves: "The size of the room, number of fitted runs, internal layout, materials and finishes, and integrated lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      We design and fit <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">bespoke wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobes</Link> and <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> for homes across Belsize Park, including Belsize Square and Belsize Village. In the villas, we can turn an attic room into a walk-in wardrobe or fit alcove units beside a chimney breast.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Villas" level={4}>Bespoke wardrobes and alcove units in Belsize Park's Victorian villas</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing={["bp-alcove-wardrobes", "bp-alcove-shelves"]}>
      <p>
        Belsize Park Gardens, Buckland Crescent and Belsize Square are lined with large semi-detached Victorian villas. If your bedroom has alcoves either side of the chimney breast, we can fit a wardrobe sized to each one. Downstairs, the living-room alcoves can take <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">low cupboards with open shelves above</Link> for books and records.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Attic rooms" level={4}>Walk-in wardrobes for attic rooms in Belsize Park</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="bp-attic-walk-in">
      <p>
        Some villas have an attic room, which can work well as a <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobe</Link>. We plan hanging space, drawers, shoe storage and shelving together, with rails where the ceiling is highest and drawers under the slope. Rail heights come from what you hang, and drawer and shelf sizes from what you fold.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Mansion flats" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes for mansion flats in Belsize Park</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing={["bp-flat-wardrobe-wall", "bp-hall-coat-cupboard"]}>
      <p>
        Belsize Park's flats include mansion blocks like Manor Mansions, and later blocks such as Hillfield Court and Tudor Close. In a flat, we can fit a wardrobe wall to wall in the bedroom, or a coat cupboard into a hall recess. You can choose Shaker doors or plain handleless ones, painted or in oak veneer.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Mews houses" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes and shelving for mews houses in Belsize Village</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing={["bp-mews-section", "bp-mews-tv-unit"]}>
      <p>
        North of Belsize Lane, mews houses on streets such as Daleham Mews have two storeys and windows on one side only. With no windows at the back, we can run a wardrobe across the full width and height of the back wall. Downstairs, the same wall can take a <Link href="/media-walls/">built-in TV unit</Link> with cupboards and shelves around it.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Wardrobes and shelving for listed homes in Belsize Park</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="bp-cornice-finish">
      <p>
        Some homes on Belsize Grove and Belsize Lane are listed, and you can check yours on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>Camden Council's listed buildings page</Link>.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If it's listed, ask the council before work starts whether the joinery needs listed building consent. That matters most if fitting it means removing or altering original skirting or panelling.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where a room still has its original cornice, we can finish the wardrobes just below it so the moulding stays visible.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Parking, lifts and access on fitting days in Belsize Park</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="bp-stair-sections">
      <p>
        We'll plan unloading, parking and the route into your home with you before fitting day. You can find your parking zone and its hours by searching your street or postcode on Camden's <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>parking zone map</Link>. Where a <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>trade permit</Link> is needed, we'll agree with you who applies for it.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In a mansion block, we'll ask you for the lift and access details and any rules the building sets for deliveries. We can also coordinate with the managing agent and provide the insurance and safety documents they ask for.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        For front steps or narrow stairs, we can bring a wardrobe in sections and assemble it in the room. Before any materials come in, we protect the floors and stairs along the route.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>The starting price of each piece, and what moves it</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. Hardware is on the manufacturer's warranty, and doors and drawers are adjusted in the first 2 years. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> shows what moves the price of a fitted wardrobe.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="What is a walk-in wardrobe called?">
        A walk-in wardrobe is also called a dressing room. We can design yours with mirrors, glass, metal details and specialist handles.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Should you have a door on a walk-in wardrobe?">
        You don't need one, as an open walk-in wardrobe keeps everything in view and easy to reach. Doors keep dust off and hide what's inside, but hinged doors need room to swing open. We can leave yours open or fit <Ask q="belsize-park-walk-in-doors">doors: which kinds you fit on a walk-in</Ask>.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What are the disadvantages of a walk-in wardrobe?">
        A walk-in wardrobe takes floor space that a wardrobe along one wall doesn't, because you need room to stand inside it. It's also a larger project, so the fitting time depends on the size of the room and the specification. With the inside on show, the interior finishes matter as much as the fronts.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
